---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { audit } from '../data/palette.mjs';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';
import { principles, comparison, requirements, automatic, PREVIEW_PAIRS } from '../data/rubric.mjs';

// Computed at build time from the same module the pre-build check runs against.
// A hand-typed table of passing numbers would be a claim; this is the check.
const rows = audit();
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.pass).length;

// The preview and the full table read the same rows, so they cannot disagree.
const preview = PREVIEW_PAIRS.map(([fg, bg]) => rows.find((r) => r.fg === fg && r.bg === bg)).filter(
  Boolean,
);

const proof = [
  ['16', 'Static pages'],
  ['0', 'Client-side scripts on content pages'],
  [`${passing} / ${rows.length}`, 'Colour pairs passing'],
  ['10', 'Old essay URLs preserved'],
];

---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I built this site the way I build products"
  metaTitle="Colophon | Tyler Young"
  description="The agent wrote the code. I defined what the site had to accomplish, what it could not compromise, and what had to be checked before it shipped."
  eyebrow="Colophon"
  meta={[
    ['Stack', 'Astro · Tailwind · static output'],
    ['Client JavaScript', 'None on content pages'],
    ['Quality gates', 'Evidence · contrast · alt text · links · open items'],
    ['Source', `<a href="${REPO}">${REPO_LABEL}</a>`],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    The agent wrote the code. I defined what the site had to accomplish, what it was
    not allowed to compromise, and what had to be checked before it could ship.
  </p>
  <ul class="cells four">
    {
      proof.map(([figure, label]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-figure">{figure}</span>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The brief came before the build</h2>
  <p>
    I wrote the standard before touching the design, so disagreements resolved
    against it rather than against taste — and while changing course was still cheap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every requirement names its verifier. Most are enforced by a script that fails
    the build. The rest are read by a person, and the page says which is which.
  </p>

  <ul class="principles">
    {
      principles.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="principle">{p.principle}</p>
          <p class="principle-detail">{p.detail}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p>
    I read the previous site end to end first: nine essays, three project cards, a
    summary, three references.
  </p>
  <p>
    The leadership argument was clear. The evidence was not. Most of it ended in a
    deck, and almost nothing could be opened.
  </p>

  <div class="ledger-compare">
    <div class="ledger-row ledger-heads" aria-hidden="true">
      <p>Before</p>
      <p>Now</p>
    </div>
    {
      comparison.map(([was, now]) => (
        <div class="ledger-row">
          <p class="was">
            <span class="tag">Before</span>
            {was}
          </p>
          <p class="now">
            <span class="tag">Now</span>
            {now}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p>
    Product work comes first because it establishes what I can make. The essays
    explain how I lead the people and systems around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Directing the agent</h2>
  <p>
    The agent wrote the implementation. I set the objective, the boundaries, the
    design system, the review criteria, and the actions that needed approval.
  </p>
  <p>
    I read every consequential change, and rejected plausible work that weakened the
    argument, introduced an unsupported fact, or cost more than it returned.
  </p>
  <p>
    Code made those decisions cheap to test. A background change is one token. A
    typography change took an unused font out of the whole build. Every change is
    checked against the same system before it ships.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">Generation made options cheaper. It did not make judgment optional.</p>

  <h2>The quality gates</h2>
  <p>
    Some things a machine can check. Some it cannot. The build draws that line.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first gate matters most. The site argues that every claim can be opened, so
    a dead deployment fails the build the same way a broken link does.
  </p>

  <ul class="ledger">
    {
      requirements.map((r) => (
        <li class:list={['gate', { human: r.mode === 'HUMAN REVIEW' }]}>
          <p class="gate-req">{r.req}</p>
          <p class="gate-meta">
            <span class="gate-mode">{r.mode}</span>
            <span class="gate-how">{r.how.replace('{pairs}', String(rows.length))}</span>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    Scripts hold the rules that can be stated exactly. People hold meaning,
    hierarchy, and whether the argument lands.
  </p>

  <h2>Contrast is computed, not assumed</h2>
  <p>
    Every pairing is checked at the smallest size it appears. It failed twice during
    the redesign. Both times the token changed rather than the component, so the fix
    propagated everywhere.
  </p>

  <p class="result-line">{passing} of {rows.length} documented colour pairs pass.</p>

  <table class="ratios">
    <caption class="sr-only">
      Four representative colour pairs, computed at build time from src/data/palette.mjs
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Pair</th>
        <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
        <th scope="col">Needs</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        preview.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                Aa
              </span>
              {r.fg} on {r.bg}
            </td>
            <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
            <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  {/* Native disclosure — the evidence stays available without dominating the page,
      and without a line of client JavaScript. */}
  <details class="all-pairs">
    <summary>View all {rows.length} colour-pair results</summary>
    <div class="scroller">
      <table class="ratios">
        <caption class="label">
          Computed at build time from <code>src/data/palette.mjs</code> — {passing} of{' '}
          {rows.length} pairs passing
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Pair</th>
            <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
            <th scope="col">Needs</th>
            <th scope="col">Used for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                    Aa
                  </span>
                  {r.fg} on {r.bg}
                </td>
                <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="use">{r.use}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>What the result proves</h2>
  <p>
    The point is not that an agent can build a portfolio. It is that a clear
    operating system turns fast generation into work someone will sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site is static, accessible and inspectable. The code is public, the claims
    connect to running work, and the checks encode standards that outlast whoever
    set them.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the problem I work on elsewhere: make intent explicit enough that people
    — and now agents — can act on it without lowering the standard.
  </p>

</DocumentLayout>
